  "data": {
    "text": "Time‐varying measures of cerebral network centrality correlate with visual saliency during movie watching\nDOI: 10.1002/brb3.2334\nBrain and Behavior (Wiley), 2021\n\nAkitoshi Ogawa1,2\n1 Faculty of Medicine, Juntendo University, Bunkyo-ku, Tokyo, Japan\n2 Brain Science Institute, Tamagawa University, Machida, Tokyo, Japan\nCorrespondence: Akitoshi Ogawa, Ph.D., Faculty of Medicine, Juntendo University, 2-1-1 Hongo, Bunkyo-ku, Tokyo, 113-8421, Japan. Email: a-ogawa@juntendo.ac.jp\n\nAbstract: This study investigated whether the visual saliency in movies was associated with network centrality. Time-varying eigenvector centrality (EC) and participation coefficient (PC) were analyzed parcel-wise during movie-watching, retinotopy, and rest; time-varying EC in parietal parcels and time-varying PC in primary sensory parcels significantly correlated with visual saliency in the movies.\n\nFunctional images were downloaded from the HCP database. The movie-watching, retinotopy task, and resting-state (both 7T and 3T images) data of 168 participants (64 males and 104 females; 22–25 years: 19; 26–30 years: 82; 31–35 years: 65; ≥ 36 years: 2) were analyzed. 7T: TR, 1000 ms; TE, 22.2 ms; Flip Angle, 45 degrees; Field of View, 208 mm × 208 mm; Voxel size, 1.6 mm isotropic; 85 slices; Multiband factor, 5; Image acceleration factor, 2; Partial Fourier, 7/8; Echo spacing, 0.64 ms; Band-width, 1924 Hz/Px. 3T: TR, 720 ms; TE, 33.1 ms; Flip Angle, 52 degrees; Field of View, 208 mm × 180 mm; Voxel size, 2.0 mm isotropic; 72 slices; Multiband factor, 8; No image acceleration; Partial Fourier, 7/8; Echo spacing, 0.58 ms; Band-width, 2290 Hz/Px.\n\nMOVIE: CC and Hollywood movies were presented (1024 × 768; 24 fps) with audio via earbuds. Run durations: 15 min 21 s (CC), 15 min 18 s (HO), 15 min 15 s (CC), 15 min 01 s (HO). Rest phases (20 s) at start and end of each run.\nRET: Six fMRI runs with retinotopic stimuli (counter-clockwise/clockwise sweeps, expanding/contracting circles, two multi-direction bar sweeps) within 16° visual angle (1024 × 768; 15 fps). Each run lasted 5 min.\n\nImage preprocessing: susceptibility-induced distortion correction; spatial normalization into MNI standard space; projection/normalization to standard surface (32,492 vertices/hemisphere); temporal high-pass filter (REST: 0.0005 Hz; MOVIE/RET: 0.005 Hz); FMRIB’s ICA-based Xnoiseifier (FIX); MSMAll multimodal surface matching. Preprocessed files are available from the HCP web page (db.humanconnectome.org, RRID:SCR_004830).\n\nNetwork analyses: A proportional 7.5% threshold was applied to correlation matrices to generate binary undirected adjacency matrices, based on the peak of the mean flow coefficient across 2.5–15% proportions. Parcellation: HCP parcellation (180 parcels/hemisphere); parcel time-series averaged across vertices. Eigenvector centrality (EC) and participation coefficient (PC) were computed from these adjacency matrices.\n\nVisual saliency: Saliency Toolbox v2.3 computed visual saliency maps (64×45) per movie frame using intensity, color, and orientation features with center–surround normalization. Mean visual saliency signal per scan was obtained by spatially and temporally averaging and then low-pass filtered at 0.1 Hz.\n\nParcel-wise relations: Temporal correlations were computed between the mean visual saliency signal and time-varying EC (tEC) and tPC (sliding window 50 s, computed every 1 s; excluding first/last 25 s). Correlation coefficients were Fisher-z transformed and Bonferroni-corrected; statistical threshold Z > 4.3 (P-FWE < 0.01).\n\nAvailability: Open access under Creative Commons Attribution License. The centrality maps and statistical results on the standard surface will be made available on reasonable request."
